indexing in list

1️⃣ What is Indexing in List?

Indexing = accessing an element using its position

a = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]


Indexes:

Positive →  0   1   2   3   4
Elements → [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]
Negative → -5  -4  -3  -2  -1

2️⃣ Positive Indexing
print(a[0])  # 10
print(a[3])  # 40

Backend:

Python jumps to index

Fetches reference

Returns object

⏱ Time Complexity: O(1)

3️⃣ Negative Indexing (Reverse Access)
print(a[-1])  # 50
print(a[-2])  # 40

How Python calculates:
a[-1]  → a[len(a) - 1]

4️⃣ IndexError (Common Mistake ❌)
print(a[5])


❌ Error:

IndexError: list index out of range


Why?

Index must be 0 to len(a)-1

5️⃣ Indexing in Nested Lists
b = [[1, 2], [3, 4], [5, 6]]


Access:

b[1]     # [3, 4]
b[1][0]  # 3

Backend:

First index → inner list reference

Second index → element reference

6️⃣ Assigning Values using Indexing (IMPORTANT 🔥)
a = [10, 20, 30, 40]
a[2] = 99

What really happens:
index 2 pointer → 30
index 2 pointer → 99


✔ Object 30 untouched
✔ Pointer changed

7️⃣ Indexing vs Slicing (Very Important)
Indexing → single element
a[2]      # 30

Slicing → new list
a[1:4]    # [20, 30, 40]


🔥 Slice always returns a new list

8️⃣ Step Value in Indexing ❌
a[::2]  # slicing


⚠ This is slicing, not indexing
Indexing never uses step

9️⃣ Using Index with Loop
for i in range(len(a)):
    print(a[i])


Better Pythonic way:

for x in a:
    print(x)

